initialization: Package initialization

A package variable is constinit, so C++ runs no dynamic initialization of its own (whose
order across files C++ does not define). A variable whose initializer is a constant, nil, or
a literal of constants is built by the C++ compiler; the others start as their zero value
and gp_var_init assigns them at program start, in Go's initialization order (dependencies
first, as go/types computes it). gp_init_funcs then calls the init functions in source
order, and gp_init_all, which main's wrapper runs, calls both. Packages are initialized
after the packages they import.

Specification: docs/spec/cpp-mapping.md#package-initialization
See also: gocpp explain order
Also known as: init, pkgvars, globals, package-variables

Example (initialization.gp):

    package main

    import "fmt"

    var limit = 100
    var table = [3]int{1, 2, 3}
    var total = compute()

    func compute() int { return limit * table[2] }

    func init() { fmt.Println("init", total) }

    func main() { fmt.Println(limit, total) }

The C++ that gocpp emit -annotate writes for it:

    //// gpkg/command-line-arguments/pkg.hpp
    // gpkg/command-line-arguments/pkg.hpp: generated by gocpp, do not edit
    #pragma once
    #include <gocpp/rt.hpp>

    namespace gpkg_main {
    int64_t compute();
    void gp_init_1();
    void main();

    extern int64_t limit;
    extern gp::array<int64_t, 3> table;
    extern int64_t total;

    void gp_var_init();
    void gp_init_funcs();
    } // namespace gpkg_main
    //// gpkg/command-line-arguments/initialization.gp.cpp
    // gpkg/command-line-arguments/initialization.gp.cpp: generated by gocpp, do not edit
    #include "gpkg/command-line-arguments/pkg.hpp"

    namespace gpkg_main {

    // ---- ./initialization.gp:9: func compute() int { return limit * table[2] }
    // gp[checks] col 42: a[i] of an array: gp::array::operator[] checks the index (gocpp build
    //     -nochecks removes it); a constant index is checked by the compiler
    int64_t compute() {
        return limit * table[2];
    }

    // ---- ./initialization.gp:11: func init() { fmt.Println("init", total) }
    // gp[initialization] col 6: func init is the C++ function gp_init_1 (a package can have several,
    //     and nothing can call them); gp_init_funcs calls them in source order after gp_var_init has
    //     set the package variables
    // gp[interfaces] col 15: fmt.Println: the runtime's gp::fmt functions take each operand with the
    //     descriptor of its Go type (gp::fmt::as), not boxed into an interface first (an operand of a
    //     non-pointer type with an Error method is, see the allocation note)
    void gp_init_1() {
        gp::fmt::Println("init"_gs, total);
    }

    // ---- ./initialization.gp:13: func main() { fmt.Println(limit, total) }
    void main() {
        gp::fmt::Println(limit, total);
    }

    } // namespace gpkg_main
